{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 3 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n<your_rewritten_code_1>\ns = input()\nn = len(s)\nmid = s[0] + s[1:n-3].replace('dot', '.') + s[n-3:]\nat_pos = mid.find('at', 1)\nprint(mid[:at_pos] + '@' + mid[at_pos+2:])\n</your_rewritten_code_1>\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\ns = input()\nn = len(s)\n\n# Find all occurrences of 'at' and 'dot' to understand the structure\n# We need to find exactly one '@' and any number of '.' in the final address\n\n# Strategy: \n# 1. Find the position of 'at' that will become '@' - it should not be at the beginning or end\n# 2. Replace 'dot' with '.' but avoid creating invalid patterns (like starting/ending with '.')\n\n# First, let's find all 'at' positions\nat_positions = []\ni = 0\nwhile i < n - 1:\n    if s[i:i+2] == 'at':\n        # Check if it's not at the very beginning or end\n        if i > 0 and i + 2 < n:\n            at_positions.append(i)\n    i += 1\n\n# Find all 'dot' positions\ndot_positions = []\ni = 0\nwhile i < n - 2:\n    if s[i:i+3] == 'dot':\n        dot_positions.append(i)\n    i += 1\n\n# Try each 'at' position as the separator\nbest_result = None\nbest_length = float('inf')\n\nfor at_pos in at_positions:\n    # Split the string into before_at and after_at parts\n    before_at = s[:at_pos]\n    after_at = s[at_pos+2:]\n    \n    # Replace 'dot' in before_at part\n    # We need to be careful about overlapping replacements\n    # Use a greedy approach: replace all non-overlapping 'dot' occurrences\n    result_before = \"\"\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(before_at):\n        if i < len(before_at) - 2 and before_at[i:i+3] == 'dot':\n            result_before += '.'\n            i += 3\n        else:\n            result_before += before_at[i]\n            i += 1\n    \n    # Replace 'dot' in after_at part\n    result_after = \"\"\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(after_at):\n        if i < len(after_at) - 2 and after_at[i:i+3] == 'dot':\n            result_after += '.'\n            i += 3\n        else:\n            result_after += after_at[i]\n            i += 1\n    \n    # Construct the email\n    email = result_before + '@' + result_after\n    \n    # Validate the email\n    # Should not start or end with '.' or '@'\n    if email[0] == '.' or email[0] == '@' or email[-1] == '.' or email[-1] == '@':\n        continue\n    \n    # Should contain exactly one '@'\n    if email.count('@') != 1:\n        continue\n    \n    # All characters should be valid (lowercase letters, '.', '@')\n    valid = True\n    for char in email:\n        if char not in 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz.@':\n            valid = False\n            break\n    if not valid:\n        continue\n    \n    # Check length and lexicographic order\n    email_len = len(email)\n    if email_len < best_length or (email_len == best_length and email < best_result):\n        best_length = email_len\n        best_result = email\n\nprint(best_result)\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.95\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-3.3378546504536644e-06, -0.0014229421503841877, -1.311301275563892e-06, -7.152555099310121e-07, -0.0038953155744820833, -5.960462772236497e-07, -2.145764938177308e-06, -0.7209765315055847, -0.40372663736343384, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -2.264974000354414e-06, 0.0, -0.008620076812803745, -2.7418097943154862e-06, -0.6468495726585388, -0.36170223355293274, -0.07550223916769028, -3.576278118089249e-07, -0.1798040121793747, -0.10023656487464905, -5.471556869451888e-05, -0.006451848894357681, -0.00014208737411536276, -5.125986263010418e-06, -0.10022275894880295, -0.5062828660011292, -0.3992518186569214, -0.4151950776576996, -0.00017975145601667464, -0.00023338454775512218, -0.0008179179858416319, -0.003548042383044958, -0.17984294891357422, -0.00023231192608363926, -0.42739370465278625, -0.27005240321159363, -3.5523738915799186e-05, -0.0012296028435230255, -9.417489309271332e-06, -0.09545053541660309, -0.8093433380126953, -8.4638240878121e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.3793368935585022, -0